@page rh="HOUSE AUREM · NAOMI · STANDING" m="TKC: TL · FR 27 · FR 29–32; VOL. I · {v1:temple} · {v1:sorted}"
# The clerk's version and the true one
rec:
On the ledger | A Vowed education student in her final year, eligible through her mother's household (TL, FR 31–32).
What she knows | Her mother was Vowed and is now House Mother. She doesn't know about 2085 (FR 30).
What's being decided for her | Whether she enters. Hannah can submit her name and isn't the only one who can, and Naomi can self-nominate once she's eligible, the way Eden did (FR 27).
What decides it | Eden. A win means Hannah enters her. A death or a public rejection means Hannah gets her out through a designation lower than the Trials reach (FR 29).
Her father | Still living, on the elite ladder below Crown level, no longer in Hannah's household. Why he left stays open (FR 31).
prompt: What a day in Vowed education looks like, hour by hour | fill

@page rh="HOUSE AUREM · NAOMI · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 1 OF 2" m="TKC: TL · CR · FR 30; VOL. I · {v1:temple} · {v1:teaches}"
# What she believes
rec:
Who teaches her | The Vowed education program (TL). The Crownward's Vowed are formed for intelligence, theological precision, and perfect devotion under close observation (CR).
What she believes | OPEN QUESTION: The files are silent. INFERENCE: She's being taught the Temple's faith by the people who'll later assess girls like her.
What she doesn't know | The cohort her mother was part of (FR 30), so her picture of the Trials is the Broadcast's.
empire: What the Temple teaches about a girl like her
POSSIBLE: A Vowed student is the Temple's own, formed to serve and to observe. If she enters the Trials, she enters as the daughter of a House Mother and a former Vowed woman, already fluent in what the Settling is watching for.
/end
prompt: What she believes about God that her instructors would mark correct, and what they wouldn't | fill

@page rh="HOUSE AUREM · NAOMI · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 2 OF 2" m="RUTH 1.20–21; 1 SAM 2.18–19; 1 SAM 3; NUM 27.1–7"
# The text
rec:
Her name | Naomi, Ruth's mother-in-law, whose name means "pleasant." Coming home widowed and childless she says, "Call me not Naomi, call me Mara," bitter (Ruth 1.20).
Where it breaks | The biblical Naomi is old and plans a younger woman's future. This Naomi is young, and her future is being planned (Vol. I, {v1:ruth}).
Samuel · 1 Samuel 2–3 | POSSIBLE: The son of a woman named Hannah, raised in the sanctuary, ministering "before the LORD, being a child," with a little coat his mother brings every year (2.18–19). **Where it breaks:** Samuel is called by name at night, and the old priest teaches him to answer. Nobody's told Naomi there's anything to answer.
The daughters of Zelophehad · Numbers 27 | POSSIBLE: Five sisters who bring their own petition and win it: "The daughters of Zelophehad speak right" (27.7). **Where it breaks:** Self-nomination is open to Naomi (FR 27). Whether she'd use it is the question.
tab:
| She'd quote | Never read to her |
| _ | Ruth 1.20, the bitterness inside her own name |
| _ | Numbers 27.7, a petition that wins |
